{
  // Typechecks the TESTS. Nothing else in this repo does, and it took two green
  // checks to notice.
  //
  // THE GAP THIS CLOSES, measured 2026-09-22 on this tree. Neither existing
  // check could see a type error in `__tests__/`:
  //
  //   `npm run build` and a bare `tsc --noEmit` use tsconfig.json, which
  //   excludes `**/*.test.ts` so a test file never lands in dist/. Confirmed
  //   with `tsc --noEmit --listFiles`: zero files under __tests__/.
  //
  //   `npm test` DOES compile every test, through ts-jest with
  //   tsconfig.jest.json, which does include __tests__. But tsconfig.json sets
  //   `isolatedModules: true`, so ts-jest takes its `transpileModule` branch
  //   rather than building a LanguageService, and that branch reports SYNTACTIC
  //   diagnostics only. A well-formed file with wrong types transpiles and runs
  //   green. Measured, not assumed: when this config was added it reported 22
  //   type errors across 4 test files that `npm test` ran 59/59 green.
  //
  //   `npm run lint` was `eslint src/` — it did not look at __tests__/ at all,
  //   and eslint here is not type-aware anyway.
  //
  // So a test could reference a name that does not exist and stay invisible. In
  // the sibling tree this was ported from that was not hypothetical: an
  // out-of-scope variable sat inside an `Array.filter` predicate, and `filter`
  // never calls its predicate on an empty array — so it would have thrown
  // ReferenceError only once the array was non-empty, which is the one case the
  // code existed to handle. Two checks that both look like coverage, and
  // neither able to see it.
  //
  // WHY THIS EXTENDS tsconfig.jest.json rather than tsconfig.json: that is the
  // program ts-jest actually compiles (`include: ["src", "__tests__"]`). Point
  // the typecheck at the same config and it checks exactly what the suite runs,
  // with one include list to maintain instead of two that drift apart. All this
  // adds is `noEmit`, because tsconfig.jest.json carries an `outDir` for
  // ts-jest's benefit and a typecheck should write nothing.
  //
  // WIRED INTO `npm run lint`, which is a departure from the sibling tree: it
  // left the equivalent config unwired because 22 pre-existing errors made
  // turning it on impossible. Those 22 were fixed here first (all in test-side
  // fakes that did not satisfy the interface they stood in for — FakeSocket.on
  // declaring one `(event: string, ...)` signature against a 4-overload
  // interface, two record() calls made against a concrete class instead of the
  // PlatformAdapter contract production uses, and an untyped `jest.Mock` whose
  // `mockResolvedValue` inferred `never`). No production type was loosened to
  // get there, which was the one shortcut worth refusing: it would have been
  // worse than the gap this closes. An unwired check reporting 22 errors is a
  // check nobody runs.
  //
  // Run it alone with `npm run typecheck:tests`.
  //
  // Verified to catch what the suite cannot: an out-of-scope name added to a
  // test inside a `filter` over an empty array is reported here as
  // `TS2304: Cannot find name`, while `npm test` stays green and counts the
  // mutant as a passing test.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.jest.json",
  "compilerOptions": { "noEmit": true }
}
